          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>What does block-level <fr:link href="/WQAM/" title="Gradient checkpointing" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/WQAM/" display-uri="WQAM" type="local">gradient checkpointing</fr:link> cost, and what does it save?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong>Saves:</html:strong> only the input to each layer is kept — one checkpoint per layer instead of the roughly twenty intermediate nodes a layer produces. In the book's setting (<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[BT = 4]]></fr:tex>M, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L = 64]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D = 8192]]></fr:tex>, bf16) that is 84 TB of activations down to 4.2 TB.

  <html:strong>Costs:</html:strong> the forward pass is run a second time inside the backward pass, so training goes from about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[6 \cdot  \mathrm {params} \cdot  \mathrm {tokens}]]></fr:tex> to about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[8]]></fr:tex> — a third more FLOPs for a twentyfold cut in activation memory.

  <html:strong>Why twenty nodes.</html:strong> <fr:link href="/SGKK/" title="Backpropagation" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/SGKK/" display-uri="SGKK" type="local">Backpropagation</fr:link> needs whatever the derivative mentions. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f(x) = \exp (g(x))]]></fr:tex> we have <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[df/dx = \exp (g(x)) \cdot  dg/dx]]></fr:tex>, so <html:em>both</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g(x)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\exp (g(x))]]></fr:tex> have to survive to the backward pass. Counting every such intermediate across a layer lands around twenty.

  <html:strong>The middle setting is usually the right one:</html:strong> save just the outputs of the big matmuls — about seven per layer — and no large matmul is ever recomputed, only the cheap elementwise work between them.
</html:details>
</html:div>



